\paragraph{An exact spectral correlation identity.} In a finite spectral window with $N$ eigenvectors, let $A$ be a self-adjoint matrix and $A(t)=e^{itH/\hbar}Ae^{-itH/\hbar}$. A direct basis expansion gives
\[
 C_N(t)=N^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}(A(t)A)
 =N^{-1}\sum_{j,k}|A_{jk}|^2e^{it(E_j-E_k)/\hbar}.
\]
For a Schwartz test function $g$ with $\widehat g(\omega)=\int g(t)e^{-i\omega t}dt$, integration yields $\int g(t)C_N(t)dt=N^{-1}\sum_{j,k}|A_{jk}|^2\widehat g((E_k-E_j)/\hbar)$. This is the precise averaged connection between correlations and squared matrix entries; it does not identify a single entry with the classical spectrum.

\paragraph{Why the Gaussian conclusion needs more.} A variance does not determine a distribution. A random variable taking $\pm\sigma$ with equal probability and a centered Gaussian of variance $\sigma^2$ have the same first two moments, but fourth moments $\sigma^4$ and $3\sigma^4$. Thus even proving the proposed second-moment scaling would not establish the diagonal Gaussian law or its symmetry factor. If $\rho(E)$ is of order $\hbar^{-d}$ and $S(0)$ stays finite, the source denominator indeed yields order $\hbar^{d-1}$ by algebra alone.

\paragraph{First missing step.} One needs a controlled quantum-to-classical correlation comparison on time/frequency scales fine enough for the shrinking near-diagonal average, followed by higher-moment or distribution estimates. The source's averaging bars, $j\ne k$ condition and $g=1,2$ assertion are all retained. No exact entrywise equality or general Gaussian theorem is claimed.

